To write the code for a Discord bot using Discord.js v14 and implementing the "ban" command as a slash command, follow these steps:

1. Install required packages: Run `npm install discord.js` in your terminal or command prompt to install the Discord.JS package.
2. Set up environment variables: Create a new.env file in the root of your project directory that contains your Discord bot's token. For example:

```
DISCORD_TOKEN=<YOUR_BOT_TOKEN_HERE>
```

Make sure you replace `<YOUR_BOT_TOKEN_HERE>` with your actual Discord Bot Token.
3. Create the Discord client event handler: Import `Client`, `Intents`, `GatewayIntentBits`, and `SlashCommandBuilder` from Discord.JS.

```javascript
import { Client } from 'discord.js';
import { Intents, GatewayIntentBits, SlashCommandBuilder } from 'discord.js';

const client = new Client({
  intents: [
   ...Intents.ALL,
    GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent
  ]
});
```

These imports will set up the Discord client object with all necessary permissions and event listeners.

4. Register an Application (Bot) on your Discord Developer Portal and add a new slash command for the "!ban" command to it with a description like "Ban a user from this server".

5. Define the command options:

```javascript
const data = new SlashCommandBuilder()
 .setName('ban')
 .setDescription('Bans a member from this guild.')
 .addUserOption(option =>
    option
     .setName('member')
     .setDescription('The member to be banned.')
     .setRequired(true)
  )
 .toJSON();
```

6. Add a listener for command interactions:

```javascript
client.on(SocketEvents.InteractionCreate, async interaction => {
  if (!interaction.isChatInputCommand()) return;

  if (interaction.commandName === 'ban') {
    const [member] = await Promise.all([
      interaction.options.getUser('member'),
      interaction.guild.members.fetch(interaction.options.getUser('member').id),
    ]);

    try {
      await interaction.member?.permissions.add(Permissions.FLAGS.BAN_MEMBERS);
      await member.ban({ days: 7, reason: `Banned by ${interaction.user.tag}` });
      await integration.reply(`Banned ${member.tag}`);

      // You can add other actions here depending on what you want to do when someone gets banned
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      await interaction.reply('There was an error banning the member.');
    }
  }
});
```

This adds a listener to the InteractionCreate event which checks whether the interaction originated from a chat input command. If its name is ‘ban’, then retrieve the mentioned member and their ID so that you can fetch them asynchronously to avoid rate limiting issues. You check for permission and also catch any potential errors caught while banning the user.

That’s it! Deploy your application to a hosting service such as Heroku, start the bot using the environment variable you created earlier, and watch it run!

