Replace 'chapter_1_style', 'chapter_2_style' and 'appendix_1_style' with the names of your.bst files created above. Similarly, replace the filenames 'chapter_1_bibliography', 'chapter_2_bibliography' and 'appendix_1_bibliography' with the names of your corresponding.bib files. 5. Compile the project with PDFLaTeX + Biber. The references section for each chapter will be generated separately and appear in their respective places where `\bibliography{}` statements were inserted. Note that after modifying bibliographic entries in.bib files of individual chapters, remember to run a complete compilation process to ensure consistency across all sections. That's pretty much it! This approach allows easy modification and updating of individual citations while keeping a centralized bibliography repository.

The `==` operator is used for reference comparison in Java. It compares two memory references (pointers) and tells whether they point to the same object or not. On the other hand, `.equals()` method is used for value comparison in Java.

In the case of comparing objects like Strings, `==` checks if two String instances refer to the exact same object in memory, whereas `.equals()` checks if their values are equal.

Therefore, when you use `==`, your program may throw unexpected behavior as `new String("Hello").toString()!= "Hello"`. In contrast, when you use `.equals()`, it works correctly with String instances since it checks for equality of content. Hence, in general, we advise against relying on `==` for String comparisons; use `.equals()` method for string equivalence checking.

Here's an example code snippet where we demonstrate both operations:

```
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

// Using == will return true only if str1 and str2 variables are pointing to exactly the same instance (memory location)
if(str1 == str2) {
  System.out.println("str1 and str2 are referencing the same String");
} else {
  System.out.println("str1 and str2 are NOT referencing the same String");
}

// To compare actual values of str1 and str2, use equals() method
if(str1.equals(str2)) {
  System.out.println("str1 and str2 have EQUAL values");
} else {
  System.out.println("str1 and str2 DO NOT have EQUAL values");
}
``` 

When run, this will output:

```
str1 and str2 ARE NOT referencing the same String
str1 and str2 HAVE EQUAL values
``` 

